<TopicPage slug="subjects/astronomy/advanced/compact-objects">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    When stars die, the fight is between <strong>gravity and the quantum rules that forbid
    squeezing</strong>. Gravity wins in stages: white dwarfs (electron pressure holds), neutron
    stars (neutron pressure holds), and finally black holes — where no known physics holds and
    the collapse, in theory, continues past where our equations can follow. These remnants are
    the universe's pressure tests for general relativity.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three remnants, and the evidence that closed the case" widget="equationStep" config={compactCfg} />

  <h2>Why compact objects matter beyond spectacle</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Type Ia supernovae:</strong> white dwarfs pushed past the Chandrasekhar limit detonate with a standardisable brightness — the "standard candles" that revealed the accelerating expansion of the universe (Nobel 2011).</li>
    <li><strong>Pulsars are nature's clocks:</strong> millisecond precision rivals atomic clocks; their timing tests gravity and may one day detect the gravitational-wave background of merging supermassive black holes (NANOGrav, 2023).</li>
    <li><strong>Neutron-star mergers forge gold:</strong> the 2017 gravitational-wave event GW170817 showed heavy elements (gold, platinum) being manufactured in the blast — the origin of your jewellery's atoms, settled by multi-messenger astronomy.</li>
    <li><strong>Black holes anchor galaxies:</strong> nearly every large galaxy hosts a supermassive one; their masses track their galaxies' bulges — a co-evolution we don't fully understand yet.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Chandrasekhar (1930); Bell Burnell (1967); Abbott et al., PRL (2017); Genzel et al. (Nobel 2020).</p>
</TopicPage>
